
A robot covers nine squares of the grid as shown in robot-on-grid.jpg.  It may be outfitted with up to nine sensors, one for each of its squares (shown in red), on the grid (but sensors are prohibitively expensive).  The robot's primitives are:

void move_forward()
void turn_left()
void turn_right()
bool sensor() - true if forward sensor is on the path, else false.

1. First determine how many sensors you need to deploy in order to traverse the green path of squares in the grid. [Answer: one forward sensor only.]
2. Design a general algorithm to perform the traversal.
3. Clearly, the termination criterion is:  there are no squares left to move to.

Assumptions:
------------
1. There will never be any cycles in the green path, i.e. next square != any previous square on the path for all next squares.
2. There will be no T-junctions in the green path.
